# Build outputs. Anchored to the root and to module roots rather than left to match any
# directory named "build": a bare "build/" also matches a Java package called build, which
# silently kept com.eonmux.excalibur.retrieval.build out of the repository and left a fresh clone
# unable to compile excalibur-api.
/build/
*/build/
*.class
*.jar
*.war
*.nar
*.zip
*.tar.gz
*.snap
*.tar
*.gz

# Gradle
.gradle/
bin/
gradle/wrapper/dists/
!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
wrapper.gradle-tasks-json

# IDE. Eclipse's project files are generated by Buildship on import and carry the classpath
# Gradle already owns, so a committed copy is a second answer to the same question that only one
# developer's workspace agrees with - and fourteen of the twenty-one modules had one, which is
# the worse half of both choices. .factorypath is the annotation-processor half of the same
# classpath, written with absolute paths into one machine's Gradle cache.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws
.classpath
.project
.settings/
.factorypath

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Logs
*.log

# Temporary files
tmp/
temp/
*.tmp
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Generated / external build layouts
**/target/
**/out/

# JMH benchmark results
**/jmh-results/
**/benchmark-results/

# Lock files
*.lock

# Maven
pom.xml.tag

# Engine runtime state. The storage engine writes segments, write-ahead logs, catalogs and
# per-segment index files into its data directory; none of it belongs in version control.
data/
*.db
*.dat
*.wal
*.idx
*.csr
*.hnsw
*.lsh

# Local configuration working copy. Track excalibur.properties.example instead; the
# working copy may hold a real EXCALIBUR_LLM_API_KEY value.
/excalibur.properties
